WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.040
Hi everyone, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners Episode 55 from May 30th, 2026.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.860
Today's AI news is pretty exciting, and I promise no jargon, let's go.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.940
So imagine you have a single photo of a quiet forest path.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:20.440
Now picture turning that still image into a world you can actually walk through using

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:21.020
your keyboard.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.480
A researcher just built a small neural network from scratch that does exactly this.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.760
The model looks at your key presses and draws the next frame right away, almost like it

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.100
is sketching each new moment as you move.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:39.880
Think of it like those old flip book animations where every page is slightly different, except

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.620
the AI creates every new page instantly based on what you just did.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.880
Instead of needing a giant data center, the whole thing runs on a regular gaming computer

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.800
with a graphics card like an RTX 5,090.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.860
This matters because it shows AI moving from just making pictures to letting you step inside them.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:03.300
For anyone who loves games or art, the line between drawing something and making it playable

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:04.560
is getting much thinner.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:10.140
Students could use this for weekend projects or quick game prototypes without a full studio.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:15.500
The current version still has rough spots like awkward motion and occasional flashes,

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.220
but a bigger model is already in training.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:23.680
You can watch the demo video on the original Reddit post and see how the model reacts to live

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:30.140
keyboard input. OK, now for my favorite part of the show, the deep dive into how this actually works.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:34.740
You know how your phone guesses the next word while you are texting a friend.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:39.560
Now imagine that same auto-complete works on pictures instead of words,

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.580
and it has to guess the next picture frame based on the last one,

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.500
plus whatever keys you just pressed.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.520
The model keeps a running memory of everything that happens so far,

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:52.600
just like how a language model remembers the conversation.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.800
Because it works one frame at a time in order,

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:00.380
it can reuse calculations from previous frames instead of starting over every time.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.700
That reuse trick is called a KV cache,

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.220
and it makes the whole thing fast enough to feel like a real game.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:11.180
So when the researcher says the network is auto-regressive,

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:15.760
it simply means that AI is always looking backward at what it just made

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.740
to decide what comes next.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:23.440
And that is basically how the model turns an ordinary photo into a living, controllable scene.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:25.340
Not so scary, right?

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.940
Now let's look at some cool stuff you can actually try.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:34.120
Google has a new AI agent called Gemini Spark that reads your emails, calendar,

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.280
and documents to help organize events.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.940
It is designed to act more like a helpful assistant that knows your whole life

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:43.680
instead of just answering one question at a time.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.440
A writer tested it by asking it to plan a birthday party

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.600
and found it still missed the most important person in the story.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:56.000
This shows how even smart agents can overlook context that feels obvious to humans.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.240
If you have access to Gemini, try giving it a simple planning task

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.840
like helping you organize a study group next week using your calendar.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.020
See what it notices or misses.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.940
It is a great way to explore where these tools shine

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:10.760
and where they still fall short.

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:14.100
Here's another quick experiment you can run right now.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.160
Open AI is sharing a life sciences model

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.360
called GPT Rosalind for free with governments and research groups.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.560
The goal is to help prepare for future pandemics

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:27.880
and early partners include big labs and vaccine groups.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:31.260
Anyone worldwide can apply to join the program,

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:36.680
which means students and curious beginners can learn how AI supports real science work.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:41.520
Doctors recently tested popular AI chatbots on medical questions

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.280
and found they gave incorrect answers about one time in five.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:49.300
The study is a reminder that these tools can sound confident

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:53.140
even when they are not accurate, especially on health topics.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:57.860
Always double check important facts with a trusted source before acting on them.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:00.000
That wraps up today's stories.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.740
The image-to-game model shows how AI is learning to create interactive worlds

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:05.860
from single pictures.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.920
Gemini Spark reminds us that personal agents still need human oversight

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.600
for the details that matter most.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:17.240
And the health chatbot results highlight why it pays to stay curious

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:18.740
and verify what you hear.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.220
If any of today's stories made you go,

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:22.180
huh, that's cool.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:23.540
Go play with it.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.040
Curiosity is how every expert started.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:27.060
See you tomorrow.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:28.960
And if you'd rather watch than listen,

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.640
find us on YouTube at At Nerra Network.

